<p><strong>What the session is about.</strong> C++/WinRT recently gained support for <strong>C++20 modules</strong>, and
the payoff is real: the Windows Terminal team adopted it and cut their build time by
roughly <strong>15%</strong> while shedding <strong>tens of gigabytes</strong> of build-time disk
usage. Ryan walks through how that support works and what it takes to adopt modules in your
own C++/WinRT code.</p>
<p><strong>What you&#8217;ll learn</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>How modules change the compilation model and why that translates into faster builds and
smaller build footprints.</li>
<li>The concrete before/after story from Windows Terminal&#8217;s adoption.</li>
<li>What you need to get started: C++/WinRT 3.0 or later and MSVC Build Tools 14.50 or later.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Why C++ developers should attend.</strong> Modules are one of the highest-leverage modernization
steps available today, but adoption still feels uncertain to many teams. Ryan turns it into
a practical, evidence-backed decision with numbers you can take back to your own project.</p>
